  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual coastal cross-section linking blue carbon ecosystems (BCEs) on the shore with nearby coral reefs offshore. On the landward side, mangroves and then seagrass-like vegetation occupy shallow sediments, with large root systems extending into the substrate. Arrows in the sediment indicate burial or storage of organic carbon and calcium carbonate, suggesting these habitats trap material belowground. Upward arrows labeled bicarbonate show dissolved carbon being exported from the vegetated coast into the water column.\n\nMoving seaward, dashed arrows marked with pH and bicarbonate indicate that the chemistry of the water is altered as it flows from mangroves and seagrasses toward the reef. The visual message is that coastal vegetated habitats can raise or modify local carbonate chemistry, apparently increasing bicarbonate availability and influencing pH in adjacent waters. This exported chemical buffering then reaches the coral reef zone, illustrated with fish and coral colonies.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes a connected seascape: mangroves and seagrass beds store carbon in sediments while also affecting surrounding seawater chemistry, potentially benefiting nearby coral reefs through linked carbon and carbonate processes.",
